   "tutor": "The rule: when a sentence has no action word, it still needs am, is or are.\nRight: \"The giraffe is very tall.\" Wrong: \"The giraffe very tall.\" The wrong sentence has no verb at all; English needs is here.\nWorked example: \"The zebra is black and white.\" First, I check for an action word: there is none. Then I look at the subject: the zebra, one animal, like it. So I choose is.\nNow use the same steps. Write am, is or are: I ___ a student. (answer with only the missing word)",
